add([1354,1357],'evaluation','Meaning of perfect training accuracy','The reproduced original notebook reaches100%training classification accuracy; what does that result establish?', ['The trained model matches the labels of this training set','The policy succeeds on every previously unseen maze','The policy is safe on a physical robot without further testing','The policy correctly navigates to every newly chosen goal'], 'Training accuracy measures agreement on the examples used for training; the other settings require separate evaluation.', ['No unseen-maze evaluation follows merely from a training score.','A discrete demonstration does not validate physical safety.','The feature vector does not even encode an arbitrary goal.'],typ='scenario',role='premise',code='train_acc computation on X_t,y_t and reproduced source run.',conflict='A successful toy-maze demonstration must not be read as evidence of general navigation performance.')
add(1354,'empty-valid','Unaddressed no-action case','If navigate reaches a cell with no legal neighboring action before reaching the goal, what does its unmodified max(valid,...) statement lack?', ['An explicit handler for an empty valid-action list','A handler for more than four possible action indices','A conversion of wall bits into floating-point values','A second hidden layer to store the goal coordinates'], 'The code calls max on valid without a guard for an empty sequence; a robust implementation must handle that case explicitly.', ['The action list is the fixed four-neighbor set.','Feature conversion does not handle an empty action list.','A hidden layer change does not itself guard the max call.'],typ='scenario',role='premise',code='navigate valid list construction and max(valid,key=lambda a:scores[a]).',assumptions=['This tests an added blocked-cell case, not a claimed failure on the original maze.'])
add(1354,'step-cap','A navigation step limit','What does max_steps=10provide in the official navigate function?', ['A cap on attempted moves, not a guarantee of reaching the goal','A guarantee that every route contains exactly10moves','A guarantee that the chosen route is the shortest available route','A cap of10training epochs rather than navigation moves'], 'The loop stops after the allowed iterations or earlier at the goal; the cap alone does not establish success.', ['The shown path reaches the goal after7moves.','A loop bound does not optimize path length.','Training uses a separate60-epoch loop.'],code='navigate loop and goal check.')
add([1354,1357],'animation','Animation frames versus navigation transitions','The notebook combines30training-animation frames with8displayed path positions; what does the total38represent?', ['Animation frames, not38robot moves','The number of wall-free cells in the maze','The number of training examples per epoch','The number of legal shortest paths proved by the notebook'], 'TOTAL_FRAMES adds30training frames and8path-position frames; the route itself has7moves.', ['The original maze contains17free cells.','There are16training examples.','The animation does not enumerate shortest paths.'],code='TRAIN_FRAMES, DRIVE_FRAMES and TOTAL_FRAMES animation constants.')
add(1358,'benefits-limits','Nonlinear mapping without a guarantee','Which statement is a defensible benefit of neural navigation models?', ['They can learn nonlinear mappings from suitable training data','They remove the need to measure behavior on new inputs','They make sensor noise irrelevant once a hidden layer is present','They produce an interpretable physical rule for each learned weight'], 'Neural models can represent nonlinear input-output relations, but performance, robustness and interpretability depend on training and design.', ['Generalization still needs evaluation.','Noise robustness is not automatic.','Individual learned weights generally do not map one-to-one to explicit physical rules.'],conflict='The slide lists advantages and limitations; benefits should not become unconditional robustness or performance claims.')
add(1358,'distribution-shift','Unseen-environment evaluation','A robot trained only in bright empty corridors is moved into a dim crowded corridor; what is the most relevant next check?', ['Test performance under the changed lighting and obstacle conditions','Report the previous training accuracy as the new deployment accuracy','Remove the new sensor readings because they differ from training','Treat unchanged network dimensions as evidence of unchanged reliability'], 'The new observations and obstacles differ from the training conditions, so deployment-relevant evaluation is needed.', ['Old training accuracy does not measure the new conditions.','Discarding changed observations does not establish successful navigation.','Fixed architecture size says nothing about performance under distribution change.'],typ='scenario',role='premise')
add(1359,'hybrid','Combining learned and conventional components','Which design best illustrates the lecture’s hybrid navigation approach?', ['A learned perception module supplying a conventional planner and safety checks','A learned image model used only to rename stored files','A controller that replaces all sensor inputs with fixed constants','A planner selected solely because its name appears first in a list'], 'A hybrid system combines learned capabilities with other planning or control components rather than assuming one method is best for every function.', ['Renaming files does not combine navigation functions in a hybrid controller.','Removing observations does not illustrate the stated combination.','List order is not a task-based design rationale.'],typ='scenario',role='premise')
add(1360,'car-tasks','Driving perception versus control','Which listed driving task is perception rather than direct vehicle control?', ['Detecting pedestrians in camera images','Applying a chosen steering command','Applying a chosen brake command','Tracking a requested wheel-speed setpoint'], 'Pedestrian detection interprets sensor data; the other examples apply or track control commands.', ['Applying steering is a control action.','Applying braking is a control action.','Tracking a wheel-speed setpoint is a control task.'],role='premise')
add(1361,'sensors','Matching sensors to measurements','Which mapping best matches the sensor table?', ['Camera: visual appearance; LiDAR: range; ultrasound: nearby obstacles; GPS: outdoor position','Camera: satellite time; LiDAR: color labels; ultrasound: global longitude; GPS: nearby echoes','Camera: body acceleration; LiDAR: language instructions; ultrasound: map identity; GPS: wheel torque','Camera: wheel speed; LiDAR: battery charge; ultrasound: image pixels; GPS: actuator current'], 'These are the sensor roles shown in the table; additional processing and operating conditions still matter.', ['The measurement sources are swapped or assigned unsupported quantities.','These are not the table’s measurement associations.','These electrical and actuation quantities do not match the listed environmental sensors.'],typ='match')
add(1361,'imu','What an IMU contributes','How should the table’s association of an IMU with orientation be interpreted?', ['IMU measurements support motion and orientation estimation, with modeling and drift considerations','An IMU directly supplies an error-free absolute global pose','An IMU identifies every object visible in a camera image','An IMU directly measures the geographic coordinates of the goal'], 'An IMU measures quantities such as angular velocity and acceleration used in state estimation; an orientation estimate is not automatically drift-free absolute pose.', ['An IMU alone is not an error-free absolute global-pose sensor.','Object recognition from images is a visual perception task.','Goal geographic coordinates are not direct IMU measurements.'],role='premise',conflict='The table compresses the IMU role into an orientation label; this does not imply perfect absolute orientation or full pose.')
add(1361,'sensor-coverage','Qualitative sensor-coverage diagram','What can be read directly from the car’s colored sensor-coverage illustration?', ['Which drawn regions correspond to camera, radar/LiDAR and ultrasonic sensing','The exact maximum range in meters of every installed sensor','The probability of detecting each pedestrian in rain','The numerical calibration error of the sensor-to-car transforms'], 'The figure identifies qualitative coverage regions by color; it does not provide numerical range, detection or calibration guarantees.', ['No metric range scale is supplied for this inference.','A schematic colored region is not a measured detection probability.','The drawing does not quantify calibration errors.'],typ='diagram',role='premise')
add(1362,'indoor-chain','Indoor perception-to-planning chain','Which sequence matches the indoor-navigation block diagram?', ['Camera input → CNN processing → object detection → safe navigation planning','Camera input → wheel torque → object detection → CNN processing','Object detection → battery charging → camera input → route deletion','Safe navigation planning → image labels → camera hardware → training-set removal'], 'The diagram places camera observations before CNN-based detection and navigation planning.', ['It places a control output before the perception stages rather than following the shown chain.','Battery charging and route deletion are not stages of the displayed chain.','This reverses the main information flow and adds unsupported operations.'],typ='diagram')
add(1363,'humanoid-fusion','Humanoid navigation around people','Why combine camera, depth or other sensor information when a humanoid moves near people?', ['The sensors can provide complementary information for perception and navigation','Their combination proves every person’s future movement exactly','Fusion converts all detected people into fixed obstacles regardless of motion','Combining sensors removes the need for motion planning'], 'Complementary sensing can improve the information available about people and surroundings; it does not remove uncertainty or planning.', ['Future human movement remains uncertain.','Moving people should not automatically be treated as static.','The robot still needs to choose safe actions or paths.'],role='premise')
add(1364,'realtime','Dynamic obstacles and response time','Why can a correct perception result still be unsuitable for navigation if it arrives too late?', ['The obstacle configuration may have changed before the robot acts','Latency changes the physical units of the predicted class labels','A delayed result always improves the current map’s accuracy','A slow model automatically produces a shorter path'], 'Dynamic surroundings can make a delayed observation stale, so response time matters alongside prediction quality.', ['Delay does not change label units.','Staleness can reduce usefulness rather than guarantee improvement.','Computation time alone does not establish shorter routes.'],typ='scenario',role='premise')
add(1365,'transformer','Vision transformers in navigation','Which role does the lecture associate with vision transformers?', ['Learning visual scene representations with relationships across image regions','Replacing all sensors with satellite-clock corrections','Converting actuator voltages directly into legal contracts','Restricting every visual decision to a single fixed pixel'], 'The slide links vision transformers to visual scene understanding and relationships across image regions.', ['Satellite-clock correction is not the listed vision-transformer role.','This is unrelated to visual navigation.','The architecture is not defined by a one-pixel-only decision constraint.'])
add(1365,'vlm','Vision-language navigation instruction','Which example best illustrates a vision-language navigation input?', ['Use the image and the instruction “go to the door beside the red chair” together','Choose a path using only a scalar battery-voltage reading','Compute wheel torque from motor current without images or language','Sort obstacle distances numerically without a scene or instruction'], 'The example combines a visual scene with a language description of the target.', ['A scalar voltage contains neither the visual scene nor the language instruction.','This is a control estimation task rather than the described vision-language pairing.','Sorting ranges does not combine visual and language information.'],typ='scenario',role='premise')
add(1365,'embodied','Embodied learning and feedback','What distinguishes embodied interaction in the lecture’s future-directions list?', ['The agent acts in an environment and learns from the resulting interactions','The agent only rearranges a fixed list of stored captions without acting','The agent replaces observations with a constant independent of actions','The agent is evaluated only by the spelling of its model name'], 'Embodied interaction connects perception and action to feedback from an environment.', ['Static caption rearrangement does not by itself provide action-environment interaction.','A constant observation independent of action removes the feedback being emphasized.','Model naming is not evidence of embodied learning.'])
add([1358,1359,1364],'design-choice','Choosing a navigation model under constraints','A robot has limited compute and must react to moving obstacles; what is the most useful comparison when selecting its navigation model?', ['Accuracy, response time, resource use and failure behavior under representative conditions','Only the number of model layers, with no timing or navigation tests','Only the largest training-set accuracy reported for another robot','Only the publication year of the architecture name'], 'The stated constraints require measured trade-offs across task performance, latency, available resources and failure modes.', ['Layer count alone does not measure the required deployment behavior.','An unrelated training score does not establish this robot’s performance.','Recency alone does not establish suitability.'],typ='scenario',role='premise')
